# Exit this page

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

Exit this page lets someone leave a page quickly when another person could see what they are reading and that would put them at risk. Use it only when research with the people affected shows that risk. A household dashboard does not need it solely because it shows personal information.

It is not a close button, a sign-out or an emergency stop. For urgent household messages, see [Show household urgency clearly](../patterns/household-urgency.html). For privacy on a screen other people can see, see [Presence, people and shared-screen privacy](../home-assistant/presence-and-privacy.html).

This page covers:

- Exit this page
  - Civica exit this page pattern

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

- A visible button with the plain label “Exit this page”, in the same place on every page of the content.
- A second keyboard route: a link that appears when it receives focus and goes to the same place.
- A neutral destination that shows nothing personal.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-civica-exit-this-page-pattern"><code>Civica exit this page pattern</code></h3>

A Civica pattern with no Home Assistant element behind it. Home Assistant has no exit control, and the Civica theme does not install one. Build it from an ordinary link or button that goes to a neutral page, together with a second link for keyboard users. Signing out or locking the device is a separate action for the device, not for this page.

The Civica theme sets no variable for this pattern. The button and links take the theme text and link colours, and a 3 pixel focus ring with a 2 pixel offset. The ring is `#0B0C0C` in light mode and `#FFDD00` in dark mode (`wa-focus-ring-color`).

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- One visible button needs no explanation.
- Going to a neutral page is quick and works without a script.
- Moving to another page does not clear the browser history, address suggestions or the device’s own records. Do not tell people that it does.
- A keyboard shortcut can clash with the shortcuts of screen readers and other assistive technology. Test with people who use them, or leave the shortcut out.
- A small preview on a page cannot prove that leaving a full page is safe.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Choose a destination that does not reveal personal content. Make the button and the secondary link go to the same place. Do not ask people to confirm; each extra step costs time they may not have.

Do not add the pattern to household dashboards without research that shows a need for it. Include people who are affected and a safety expert in that research.

Success criteria that apply:

- 2.1.4 Character Key Shortcuts: a shortcut made of a single character must be possible to turn off or change. Prefer a press of more than one key, or no shortcut.
- 2.4.1 Bypass Blocks and 2.4.7 Focus Visible: the keyboard route can be reached and the focus can be seen.
- 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured (Minimum): the focused link is not hidden.
- 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum): the button is large enough to press quickly.
- 3.2.2 On Input: the button behaves as its label says.
- 2.2.1 Timing Adjustable: nothing on the page depends on a time limit.

A plain label, a fixed position and no extra steps keep the load low.

<h3 id="check">Check it works</h3>

Test the visible button, the secondary link, any shortcut, slow loading, keyboard focus, a page with scripts turned off and the browser Back button. Do this on the real page, not on a preview. See [Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html).

